# Nilzora mobile — local environment template.
# Copy to apps/mobile/.env (git-ignored) and restart `expo start`. Placeholders only.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_* values are inlined into the app bundle at build time, so anyone with the
# app can read them. Never give a secret (API keys, service-role keys, client secrets)
# an EXPO_PUBLIC_ name. Clients reach Supabase only through the Nilzora API, so no
# Supabase URL or key belongs here.
#
# Session tokens are never configured: after sign-in they are kept only in the device
# keychain/keystore (Expo SecureStore), never in AsyncStorage or files.

# The only setting: address of the Nilzora API (apps/api). In development, localhost means
# "the computer running expo start": the app swaps it for the address the phone (Expo Go or
# a dev build) already reached for the bundle, so it keeps working when the laptop's Wi-Fi
# address changes (on the Android emulator use http://10.0.2.2:4000 instead). A LAN IP
# (e.g. http://192.168.0.10:4000) is used exactly as written and
# must be the laptop's current one. Plain http is accepted only in development builds;
# release builds require https and are never rewritten.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4000

# Android remote push (development builds only; Expo Go cannot receive it). Path to the
# Firebase google-services.json, which is git-ignored and never committed. Unset, the app
# still builds and reports order updates as unavailable instead of registering for push.
# GOOGLE_SERVICES_FILE=./google-services.json

# Google Sign-In on Android (needed only once the native module is in the build). This is the
# *web* client id from Google Cloud — the same value as GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID in apps/api/.env —
# which the app hands to the Google Sign-In SDK so the returned ID token is addressed to this
# API: the API checks that id as the token audience. It is public (it ships inside the app
# bundle), so never put the client *secret* here. Left unset, the app still builds, the Google
# button stays hidden, and email/password sign-in is unaffected.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=1234567890-abc.apps.googleusercontent.com
# The customer storefront's public address, so the app can put a real product link on the
# Android share sheet. Public information (it ships inside the app bundle). Leave it unset and
# the Share action is simply not shown — the app never guesses an address. In development the
# phone must reach the storefront too, so use the laptop's LAN address rather than localhost,
# e.g. http://192.168.0.10:3000
EXPO_PUBLIC_STOREFRONT_URL=
